==German==

===Etymology===
{{root|de|ine-pro|*dewk-}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|ziehen}}, from {{inh|de|goh|ziohan}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*teuhan}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*teuhaną}}, from {{der|de|ine-pro|*dewk-||to pull, lead}}.

Cognate with {{cog|nds|tehn}}, {{cog|got|𐍄𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽}}, {{cog|dum|tīen}}, {{cog|ang|tēon}}, {{cog|non|toga}}, {{cog|fy|tsjen}}. Compare obsolete {{cog|en|tee||to draw, lead, proceed}} and from the same root as English {{m|en|tie}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈt͡siːən/|/t͡siːn/}}
* {{audio|de|De-ziehen.ogg}}
* {{audio|de|De-ziehen2.ogg}}
* {{audio|de|De-ziehen3.ogg}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb|ziehen<zog,gezogen,zöge.haben,sein>}}

# {{lb|de|transitive|or|intransitive}} to [[pull]]; to [[drag]] {{+aux|de|haben}}
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[draw]] (e.g. a weapon); to [[extract]]; to [[puff]] {{+aux|de|haben}}
# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[draw]] (a conclusion, lesson, etc.) {{+aux|de|haben}}
#* {{quote-journal|de|year=2010|journal=w:Der Spiegel|url=http://www.spiegel.de/spiegel/print/index-2010-25.html|issue=25/2010|page=77
|passage=Es gilt deshalb, die richtigen Lehren aus der Krise zu '''ziehen''', aus den Fehlern der Vergangenheit zu lernen, um die Zukunft zu sichern.|t=Therefore it is necessary to draw the right lessons from the crisis, to learn from the mistakes of the past for securing the future.}}
# {{lb|de|impersonal|intransitive}} to be [[drafty]]; there to be a [[draft]] {{gloss|current of air}}
#: {{uxi|de|Fenster zu! Es '''zieht'''!|Shut the window! There’s a draft!}}
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[move]]; to [[migrate]] {{+aux|de|sein}}
#: {{ux|de|Ich '''ziehe''' nach Hamburg, aber mein Bruder '''zieht''' in eine andere Stadt.|I'm '''moving''' to Hamburg, but my brother is '''moving''' to another city.}}
# {{lb|de|intransitive}} to [[roam]]; to [[head]] {{+aux|de|sein}}
# {{lb|de|reflexive}} to [[stretch]]; to [[warp]] {{+aux|de|haben}}
# {{qualifier|on a door handle}} pull
#: {{ant|de|ziehen|t1=to push}}

====Conjugation====
{{de-conj|ziehen<zog,gezogen,zöge.haben,sein>}}
* In early modern German, the 1st, 2nd and 3rd persons singular present and the imperative singular were alternatively ''ich'' {{m|de|zeuch}}, ''du'' {{m|de|zeuchst}}, ''er'' {{m|de|zeucht}}, ''{{m|de|zeuch}}''!

